A vararg slot is a register, not a sizeof.

emit_va_arg_intrinsic advanced ap by sizeof(ty). The READ is at the type's
width and that is right; the STRIDE is not. Every variadic argument occupies a
full register-width slot on this ABI whatever its declared type, so advancing
by sizeof lands mid-slot for anything narrower and desynchronises every
argument after it.

While `int` was eight bytes this was invisible: int, long and every pointer
were all register-width. At four bytes the first %d corrupts the rest.

    snprintf(b, 64, "%d %d", 1, 2)          returned 12, glibc 3
    snprintf(b, 64, "%d %d %d", 1, 2, 3)    returned 14, glibc 5
    snprintf(b, 64, "%s%d%s", "a", 2, "c")  SIGSEGV, glibc 3
    snprintf(b, 64, "%s%s%s", ...)          correct -- char* is register-width

That last line is the tell: %s survived and %d did not, because pointers happen
to still be a register wide and int no longer is.

WHAT IT COST. tcc's pp_error formats "%s:%d: ..." so every diagnostic it tried
to print came out corrupt, and the garbage sent cstr_vprintf into a loop that
never terminated. The tcc micro-c builds hung instead of compiling, and the
hang was three layers away from the cause: vsnprintf at 74% of the trace,
reached through pp_error, with the real defect in the compiler's varargs.

A type WIDER than a register keeps its own size, so a struct passed by value
still advances by its own width.

MEASURED: every case above now matches glibc exactly. difftest stays 84/85 --
no regression -- and the 51 vsnprintf checks still pass.

AND IT UNBLOCKS THE COMPILER. mc-tcc now compiles correctly: `-c` produces a
1,038-byte object, and the freestanding hello world it builds RUNS and prints
"hello, world". It still hangs at exit rather than terminating -- the work is
done and something in teardown spins -- so this is not the last bug, but it is
the one between "hangs instead of compiling" and "compiles, then hangs".

